speechtext:
That
involves the establishment of shipping facilities by the National Transcontinental; that is another phase' of the subject with which I may deal. At the moment I am dealing entirely with this question of rates, the subject of this resolution and the question that has been largely discussed this afternoon. I am pointing out that the obligation which rests upon the National Transcontinental Railway to-day in regard to rates is that where traffic is not specially routed, it must be carried to Canadian ports. Now what has occurred in regard to that? The Canadian Northern Railway was taken over by the government of the day in 1918. I opposed the taking over of the Canadian Northern railway when I was in parliament, both in 1914 and in 1917. I think this country should not have been saddled with that enormous railway obligation, but if possible the railway should have gone into the hands of a receiver. However, all that is past history; we did take over the road and it became incorporated with the National Transcontinental, and later, in 1919, with the Grand Trunk. That was the situation in 1919 when the Intercolonial Railway first came under the control of the same governing forces as controlled the Canadian Northern and the Grand Trunk.
In order that the record may be clear, I may point out that when the government of the day in 1919 came to take over the Grand Trunk Railway there was no provision made there by which these pledges, these statutory arrangements, would be confirmed and carried out. My hon. friend from Quebec West (Mr. Parent) at that time, on the third reading of the bill to take over the Grand Trunk railway, moved:
That the said bill be not now read a third time, but that the House do come to the following resolution:
That the Grand Trunk Railway System which the government proposes to acquire, comprises many miles of railway lines on American soil;
That the Grand Trunk Railway Company has diverted most of its traffic from Canadian ports to its United States terminals;
That a continuance of such policy by the Canadian government would be injurious to Canadian trade and detrimental to our national welfare;
That the ownership by the Canadian government of the American section of the Grand Trunk railway would involve this country in international troubles respecting labour administration and public policy;
That for the above-nfentioned reasons, the acquisition of these lines means a serious danger of the annexation of Canada by the United States;

2322	COMMONS
Maritime Rights-Mr. Macdonald (Antig.)
That the House is of opinion that before taking action towards the acquisition of the railway referred to the government should give an immediate assurance to this House that it does not intend to divert Canadian trade from Canadian ports by taking over that portion of the Grand Trunk railway situated and operated in the United States.
Well, the Grand Trunk was taken over, and the great complaint in the Maritime provinces has been that the trade from the west has gone to Portland, the American terminus of the line. Now that is a question of fact. There is no doubt about the obligations contained in the statute. This government freely and unreservedly assumes the obligations and regards them as binding on the Canadian National Railway Company. It is a question of fact as to how far these obligations are not being carried out. It is all very well for hon. gentlemen to say that the freight is not going as it should. This House naturally wants to have some definite and clear finding on the question whether or not this obligation is being discharged.
